"""
paper_section_2.py
Section 2: RELATED WORK, comprehensive scholarly literature review, and Research Gap.
"""

from paper_common import add_p, add_h

def build_section_2(doc):
    add_h(doc, "2. RELATED WORK", 1)
    
    # 2.1 Crop Yield Prediction (~500 words, 8 papers)
    add_h(doc, "2.1 Crop Yield Prediction", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "Forecasting agricultural production has been a central pursuit of agronomic science, agricultural economics, and earth system modeling for over a century. Classical approaches to crop yield estimation originated with process-based biophysical crop growth simulation models, such as the Decision Support System for Agrotechnology Transfer (DSSAT) and the Agricultural Production Systems sIMulator (APSIM) [5]. Process-based simulators model physiological crop phenology, organ development, soil water dynamics, carbon assimilation, and nitrogen uptake by numerically integrating daily meteorological data and plant physiological parameters [5, 6]. While biophysical simulators provide valuable mechanistic interpretability and enable counterfactual climate scenario modeling, their real-world scalability across continental and international agricultural domains is severely constrained by their extreme data requirements. Parameterizing DSSAT or APSIM requires high-resolution daily solar radiation, detailed soil profile hydrology (e.g., wilting points, saturation moisture capacity, soil organic carbon), cultivar-specific genetic coefficients, and precise field management schedules (e.g., exact sowing dates, fertilizer application timings, and irrigation depth) [1, 5]. In the vast majority of agricultural regions globally—particularly across smallholder farming systems in developing nations—such localized micro-data are unavailable, leading to substantial calibration errors when simulators are scaled up [1, 4]."
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "To mitigate the calibration bottlenecks of biophysical modeling, empirical agro-meteorological statistical models emerged as practical alternatives. Pioneering global-scale investigations by Lobell and Field [30] utilized historical county and national time-series data to quantify statistical relationships between growing-season temperature, precipitation anomalies, and national yields for major food staples, demonstrating that rising temperatures have already suppressed global yield gains for wheat and maize. Extending this empirical foundation, Schlenker and Roberts [31] analyzed non-linear thermal responses using high-resolution weather grids in the United States, uncovering sharp asymmetric threshold effects: crop yields increase modestly with temperature up to 29°C for corn and 30°C for soybeans, but decline precipitously when temperatures exceed these critical physiological tipping points. Similarly, Ortiz-Bobea et al. [3] conducted a comprehensive econometric assessment showing that anthropogenic climate change has slowed global agricultural total factor productivity growth by approximately 21% since 1961, with tropical agricultural systems suffering disproportionate yield penalties. In a comprehensive review of emerging modeling trends, Bali and Singla [4] highlighted that while classical linear regression and agro-meteorological indices capture aggregate secular trends, they exhibit high residual variance during extreme climate anomalies due to complex multi-factor interactions between moisture deficits, heat stress, and pest pressure. Concurrently, Abrougui et al. [6] compared multiple linear regression against artificial neural networks for organic potato yield modeling under diverse tillage regimes, demonstrating that empirical non-linear formulations consistently outperform rigid linear models by adapting to localized soil physical characteristics. Furthermore, systematic surveys by van Klompenburg et al. [7] confirmed that large-scale agricultural yield forecasting is rapidly transitioning from classical parametric statistics toward data-driven machine-learning algorithms capable of ingesting diverse, heterogeneous environmental variables without imposing restrictive functional assumptions."
    )
    
    # 2.2 Machine Learning Approaches Relevant to This Study (~600 words, 18 papers)
    add_h(doc, "2.2 Machine Learning Approaches Relevant to This Study", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "Driven by expanding agricultural databases, remote sensing platforms, and computational capabilities, machine learning has emerged as the dominant methodological paradigm for empirical crop-yield prediction. A diverse array of algorithmic families has been explored in agricultural benchmarks, spanning parametric linear models, non-parametric instance-based learners, tree ensembles, and artificial neural networks [4, 7, 22]. Foundational work in pattern recognition by Cover and Hart [27] established the asymptotic error bounds of K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), proving that the nearest-neighbor decision rule possesses an asymptotic error rate bounded by twice the Bayes risk. In agricultural analytics, KNN has been widely applied for yield forecasting, soil spatial interpolation, and sensor management; for example, Zaki et al. [14] demonstrated that optimized KNN algorithms achieve superior convergence and localization in distributed agricultural sensor networks. Furthermore, El-Kenawy et al. [2] conducted an extensive empirical benchmark for potato crop yield prediction, systematically evaluating KNN, Gradient Boosting Regressors, XGBoost, and Multilayer Perceptrons (MLP) alongside deep learning architectures, demonstrating that properly tuned distance-weighted KNN models can compete favorably with, or even surpass, complex architectures on medium-sized tabular datasets when features are appropriately standardized. Mishra et al. [23] reached similar conclusions when forecasting potato production across major South Asian nations, observing that distance-based and non-parametric regressors frequently exhibit superior local calibration compared to classical ARIMA time-series models."
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "Concurrently, tree-based ensemble methods have achieved widespread prominence due to their natural ability to handle mixed data types, high-dimensional categorical features, and non-linear interactions without requiring strict distributional assumptions [7, 26]. Breiman [26] established the theoretical foundation of Random Forests, demonstrating that aggregating randomized decision trees substantially reduces prediction variance while preserving low bias. Expanding tree-based architectures into stage-wise optimization, Friedman [15] introduced Gradient Boosting Machines (GBM), which fit successive shallow regression trees to the pseudo-residuals (negative loss gradients) of the preceding ensemble, delivering exceptional regression accuracy on tabular data. Chen and Guestrin [16] advanced the boosting paradigm through Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), incorporating second-order Taylor approximations of the objective loss function, explicit L1/L2 leaf weight regularization, sparsity-aware split finding, and hardware-accelerated tree pruning. In agricultural applications, Noorunnahar et al. [8] deployed XGBoost to forecast annual rice production in Bangladesh, establishing that tree ensembles effectively untangle complex precipitation-temperature interactions while resisting overfitting. Similarly, Cedric et al. [9] benchmarked machine-learning models across West African agro-ecological zones, demonstrating that gradient-boosted tree architectures achieve robust cross-regional yield estimates. In the US Midwest, Kang et al. [10] conducted a comparative assessment of environmental predictors and regression algorithms for county-level maize yield prediction, finding that tree ensembles consistently outperformed linear baselines by capturing saturation thresholds in rainfall and solar radiation. Patnaik and Padhy [11] reinforced these findings in potato yield studies, demonstrating that gradient boosting provides high explanatory fidelity across diverse fertilizer application regimes."
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "Neural network architectures and deep learning models have also received extensive investigation. Originating from the error backpropagation learning algorithm formulated by Rumelhart et al. [34], Multilayer Perceptrons (MLPs) function as universal non-linear function approximators. Ahmed [17] developed a software framework incorporating modified MLPs for regional maize yield prediction, demonstrating that neural networks can learn intricate feature representations when provided with extensive training iterations. Expanding neural architectures to spatial and temporal dimensions, Cao et al. [19] integrated Google Earth Engine satellite imagery with deep neural networks for multi-scale wheat yield forecasting in China, showing that deep learning captures hierarchical spectral-phenological patterns. Similarly, Fan et al. [24] formulated hybrid Graph Neural Networks and Recurrent Neural Networks (GNN-RNN) to model geospatial dependencies and temporal yield trajectories across US agricultural counties, while Mateo-Sanchis et al. [25] utilized interpretable Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to capture sequential seasonal dependencies from satellite vegetation observations. However, as Paudel et al. [5, 12] observed across multi-national European yield benchmarking studies, deep neural networks often struggle to outperform shallow tree ensembles or instance-based models on tabular datasets of moderate sample size, where deep networks are susceptible to overfitting and local optima [4, 7]."
    )
    
    # 2.3 Seasonal and Regional Crop-Yield Modeling
    add_h(doc, "2.3 Seasonal and Regional Crop-Yield Modeling", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "A critical yet frequently overlooked dimension in agricultural machine learning is the biophysical and physiological distinction between cropping seasons. Global agriculture operates within distinct seasonal cultivation windows characterized by divergent hydrological and thermal regimes. In South Asia and comparable tropical/subtropical agro-ecological zones, the Kharif season coincides with the southwest monsoon (June to October), characterized by high cumulative precipitation, high humidity, and warm growing temperatures suitable for C4 cereal crops such as maize and sorghum [9, 10]. In contrast, the Rabi season occurs during cooler, post-monsoon winter months (November to April), relying predominantly on controlled irrigation, residual soil moisture, and moderate thermal conditions essential for C3 temperate cereals such as wheat [19, 23]. Furthermore, multi-season or perennial crops—including root and tuber staples such as potatoes and cassava—span diverse cultivation intervals and yield vast harvested fresh biomass per hectare [2, 11]. When researchers train a single pooled regression model across all available records, the model is forced to fit a single set of coefficients or decision splits across crops with fundamentally different physiological yield potentials and water sensitivities. Developing season-specific pipelines isolates these contrasting mechanisms, allowing models to learn specialized functional relationships tailored to each seasonal regime."
    )
    
    # 2.4 Temporal Validation in Agricultural Prediction
    add_h(doc, "2.4 Temporal Validation in Agricultural Prediction", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "A major methodological vulnerability in machine-learning literature across empirical sciences is evaluation data leakage. In their seminal taxonomy of data mining failures, Kaufman et al. [28] defined leakage as the inadvertent introduction of spurious information about the target variable that would not legitimately be available at operational prediction time. Recently, Kapoor and Narayanan [29] conducted a broad reproducibility audit across machine-learning studies, identifying evaluation data leakage—specifically improper cross-validation and feature scaling across partition boundaries—as a primary driver of non-reproducible performance claims. In agricultural yield forecasting, this issue is acute when researchers apply standard randomized K-fold cross-validation or random train-test splitting [7, 10]. Because agricultural databases are structured longitudinally across years and geographic regions, a random split places harvest records from the same country and adjacent years (e.g., 2011 and 2013) on both sides of the training and test divide. The model can thus interpolate yield from future records to predict past observations, bypassing the actual challenge of prospective forecasting. To prevent temporal leakage, Roberts et al. [32] and Paudel et al. [5] emphasized the imperative of block cross-validation and prospective out-of-time chronological splitting, wherein models are trained strictly on past historical years and evaluated on unobserved future crop seasons."
    )
    
    # 2.5 Explainable and Interpretable Agricultural Machine Learning
    add_h(doc, "2.5 Explainable and Interpretable Agricultural Machine Learning", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "As machine-learning models transition from academic research into operational agricultural decision support, algorithmic transparency and explainability have become essential prerequisites for stakeholder adoption [4, 22]. Black-box predictions that fail to articulate underlying agronomic drivers can result in farmer skepticism or misguided policy interventions. Addressing this challenge, Lundberg and Lee [21] formulated SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), a game-theoretic framework unifying local feature attribution methods based on Shapley values. Lundberg et al. [35] subsequently developed TreeSHAP, enabling polynomial-time exact Shapley computation for tree ensembles. In environmental and atmospheric predictive modeling, Gupta et al. [13] demonstrated that incorporating SHAP feature attributions into stacked ensemble architectures illuminates complex non-linear feature interactions and establishes trust in critical predictions. In non-parametric instance-based models such as KNN, where tree-based split gains are absent, permutation feature importance and local neighborhood distance deconstruction provide rigorous model-agnostic alternatives for auditing algorithmic sensitivity [7, 21]. Furthermore, Chai and Draxler [18] and Bergstra and Bengio [33] emphasized that thorough empirical evaluation requires assessing multiple complementary performance metrics (RMSE, MAE, R², Pearson r) alongside systematic hyperparameter optimization to ensure that reported model performance reflects genuine inductive capability rather than arbitrary tuning configurations."
    )
    
    # 2.6 Research Gap (3-4 points)
    add_h(doc, "2.6 Research Gap", 2)
    add_p(doc, 
        "Despite substantial advancements in agricultural machine learning, a comprehensive synthesis of existing literature reveals four critical research gaps that motivate our investigation:"
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "1. Pervasive Season-Blind Pooled Modeling: The vast majority of published international crop-yield studies construct monolithic regression models that pool diverse crops across distinct growing seasons. This practice overlooks the fundamental agro-ecological divergence between monsoon-dependent Kharif crops, winter-irrigated Rabi cereals, and high-yield Seasonal perennials, diluting predictive precision and obscuring season-specific climatic sensitivities.",
        bold_prefix="1. Season-Blind Monolithic Modeling: "
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "2. Evaluation Leakage via Randomized Partitioning: A large proportion of agricultural yield papers report headline accuracies derived from randomized train-test splits. By failing to enforce a strict chronological holdout, these studies allow temporal data leakage, masking performance degradation caused by real-world temporal distribution shifts and resulting in overly optimistic generalization claims.",
        bold_prefix="2. Evaluation Data Leakage via Random Partitioning: "
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "3. Absence of Historical Neighborhood and Reliability Deconstruction: While instance-based algorithms such as KNN are occasionally benchmarked, literature rarely explores the mechanisms behind their performance. Specifically, existing studies lack rigorous audits of historical nearest-neighbor distances, neighborhood target dispersion, prediction intervals, and feature group ablation sensitivities.",
        bold_prefix="3. Lack of Neighborhood Deconstruction and Uncertainty Auditing: "
    )
    add_p(doc, 
        "4. Practical Disconnect Between Offline Models and Production Deployment: Most agricultural machine-learning research terminates at offline Jupyter notebooks or static script evaluations. There is a marked absence of end-to-end architectures that translate calibrated multi-season ML pipelines into operational, authenticated, RESTful software systems featuring database audit logging and interactive user interfaces.",
        bold_prefix="4. The Deployment Divide in Agricultural Decision Support: "
    )

print("paper_section_2.py created.")
